'' dllimplib.ti -- an import library for a DLL that came without one (book work item T98).
''
'' lld-link cannot link a .dll: it links the import library that names the DLL's exports.
'' A '#foreignlib NAME dynamic' spec may still find only the DLL -- SQLite's Windows
'' download is sqlite3.dll and a .def with no LIBRARY line, and nothing else -- and that
'' file went to the linker as it was: 'bad file type. Did you specify a DLL instead of an
'' import library?'. On windows-x86_64 the link now makes the import library itself: this
'' file reads the DLL's export table into the text of a .def, and toolchain.tiko's
'' MakeImportLib hands that to llvm-dlltool, which ships beside clang.
''
'' THE DLL IS NAMED AS IT NAMES ITSELF -- the name in its export directory, which is the
'' name it was built as -- and not by the file's name. That is the counterpart of a
'' soname: a copy renamed for the link ('sqlite3_windows_x86_64.dll', for '#foreignlib's
'' target tags) still makes a program that loads 'sqlite3.dll'. A DLL with no name there
'' is named by its file.
''
'' DATA OR CODE, per export: an export whose address lies in a section that holds no code
'' is written 'DATA', as build/mkmsvcdef.py decides for the toolchain's own import
'' libraries -- a data export has no call thunk. A forwarder (an address inside the export
'' directory itself) is code. Exports with no name, by ordinal only, are left out: no C
'' declaration can name them.

#ifndef __TKO_DLLIMPLIB_BI__
#define __TKO_DLLIMPLIB_BI__

#include once "tko.ti"

namespace Tko.Codegen

    '' The text of a .def for 'dll': 'LIBRARY <name>', 'EXPORTS', one line per named
    '' export. "" with 'why' set when the file is not a 64-bit x86-64 DLL with exports.
    declare function DllDefText( byref dll as string, byref why as string ) as string

end namespace

#endif
